<TopicPage slug="subjects/classics/foundational/homer-and-the-world-of-the-hero">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Classics begins for most students with Homer — and with good reason. The Iliad and
    the Odyssey, composed within a centuries-old singing tradition and written down at
    some point around the eighth or seventh century BCE, are the founding texts of
    Western literature: the first surviving, fully achieved studies of rage, grief,
    homecoming and fame. "The world of the hero" is doubly the subject — a physical
    setting (Bronze Age palaces and camp-fires, recollected through a later "dark age")
    and a moral universe with its own currency. Honour is what heroes earn, exchange and
    die for; glory is what survives them; and the two epics are the tradition's first
    great audit of that economy, one concluding it is bankrupt, the other auditing what
    survives when the currency is gone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Neither poem is what a newcomer expects. The Iliad is not the story of the Trojan
    War (it never mentions the wooden horse) but of a few weeks in its tenth and final
    year — a quarrel between two Greek commanders that escalates into tragedy. The
    Odyssey is not a straightforward adventure but a homecoming told in flashes, where
    the monsters matter less than whether a marriage can survive twenty years of
    absence. And both poems are performances as much as texts — built from repeated
    formulae by singers who composed in real time, which is why the first scholarly
    question ("who was Homer?") was only answered by understanding how oral poetry
    works. This chapter takes the three parts in order: the Iliad's rage, the Odyssey's
    homecoming, and the technique of the songs themselves.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two epics, one heroic universe" widget="equationStep" config={epicCfg} />

  <h2>The Iliad: rage and heroism</h2>
  <p>
    The Iliad's first word is <em>menin</em> — rage, wrath — and the poem is organised by
    it. Agamemnon, commander of the Greek forces, seizes Briseis, Achilles' war-prize
    and companion; Achilles, the greatest warrior alive, is dishonoured, withdraws from
    the fighting, and asks his divine mother Thetis to have Zeus turn the war against
    his own side until the Greeks learn his worth. The plot is thus barely a war story:
    it is the anatomy of one man's anger and the price everyone else pays for it. The
    Greeks bleed; an embassy of old friends offers Achilles lavish compensation (Book 9,
    one of the poem's summit scenes); Achilles refuses — what use is honour from people
    who took it? — until Patroclus, his dearest companion, wears Achilles' armour into
    battle and dies at Hector's hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    The heroic code explains why the quarrel is unanswerable. A hero's worth is
    <em>time</em> — honour, the visible respect paid in goods and words — and it is a
    finite currency: praise given to another is praise not given to you. Achilles had
    been given a choice of two fates: a long life at home with no glory, or a short
    life at Troy with fame (<em>kleos</em>) forever. He came to Troy knowing this. The
    poem is what the choice costs: rage returns him to battle after Patroclus' death, he
    kills Hector and desecrates the body, and the poem ends not with victory but with
    the most famous scene of shared grief in Western literature — the old king Priam
    coming alone by night to kiss the hands of his son's killer, and the two enemies
    weeping together, "one for a father, one for a friend." Homer invented, in the very
    first texts we have, the idea that a war story could be a tragedy of the victors —
    and the poem keeps its power because its battlefield is rendered without glory's
    varnish: the spear "caught him beside the eye," the dogs, the dust, the widows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two scenes do the poem's deepest work. Book 6 gives Hector, the Trojan champion and
    the poem's most human hero, his farewell on the city wall: his wife Andromache
    begs him to stay, his baby son is frightened by the helmet's horsehair plume, and
    Hector laughs, removes it, and takes the boy up — before walking back to a battle
    he knows he will lose, because honour leaves him no choice the poem could respect.
    And the gods, who seem at first like fairy-tale machinery, function as a sharp
    commentary on the heroic code: Zeus watches mortals die for honour the gods
    manipulate for amusement, and the poem lets the mismatch stand — the heroes'
    seriousness measured against a divine audience that has none. The Iliad is thus
    simultaneously the greatest advertisement for the heroic ideal and its most
    corrosive critique, which is exactly why the tradition never stopped arguing with it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the Iliad tells the Trojan War, ending with the
    wooden horse. The horse belongs to other traditions (and appears in the Odyssey as
    a story told at a feast); the Iliad ends with Hector's funeral — the war unfinished,
    as everyone in the audience knew it must be. The poem's selectivity is its art: by
    choosing four weeks of a ten-year war and one hero's anger as its subject, it makes
    the war a lens for mortality rather than a sequence of events.
  </p>

  <h2>The Odyssey: homecoming and cunning</h2>
  <p>
    The Odyssey opens where war epics close: with survival as the problem. Odysseus is
    stranded a decade after Troy, held on an island by a nymph who offers him
    immortality — the heroic currency revalued as a trap — while back home his house
    fills with suitors consuming his estate and pressing his wife Penelope to remarry.
    The poem's structure sets its values: the first four books follow the son,
    Telemachus, learning to act; the central books let Odysseus narrate his own
    wanderings (the Cyclops Polyphemus, blinded by the trick of the name "Nobody";
    Circe; the Sirens, survived by being lashed to the mast; the land of the dead);
    the last twelve deliver the return in disguise, the bow contest, and the slaughter
    and reunion the whole poem has been aimed at.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where Achilles' excellence is force, Odysseus' is <em>metis</em> — cunning
    intelligence, the trick that wins when strength cannot: endure the Cyclops' cave
    until escape is possible, lie his way through his own hall, weep convincingly at
    his own story told by others. And the poem's moral centre is domestic. The Odyssey
    audits the heroic world from the household's point of view: fidelity is tested not
    on battlefields but in bedrooms and weaving rooms — Penelope, unravelling her
    shroud nightly for three years to stall the suitors, is the poem's quiet hero, and
    the final test of identity is the marriage bed, rooted to a living olive tree, that
    only the true husband could know was moved. Hospitality (<em>xenia</em>) is the
    poem's working ethic: every door tells you whether a society is civilised by how it
    treats the stranger at the threshold, and the Cyclops — who eats his guests — is
    the counter-case that defines the value.
  </p>
  <p>
    The poem's return is deliberately double: Odysseus regains his house in a night of
    slaughter, and the modern reader gets a second, quieter test in the aftermath —
    the hanging of the disloyal maidservants, ordered without ceremony, sits in the
    poem without comment and refuses to sit in the reader without discomfort. That
    friction is productive, not a flaw: the Odyssey holds up the heroic world's own
    mirror and lets a contemporary audience see both the grandeur and the cruelty the
    code priced in. Later ages have read the ending as triumph, as horror, and as both
    at once — which may be the most honest way an epic about homecoming can close:
    the hero is home; what the return cost, the poem does not let you uncount.
  </p>

  <h2>Epic technique and the oral tradition</h2>
  <p>
    The epics' strangest surface feature — the repetition — is the key to how they were
    made. "Swift-footed Achilles" appears even when he is sitting down; "rosy-fingered
    Dawn" and "the wine-dark sea" return book after book; arming scenes, feasts and
    sacrifices unfold in standard blocks. These are not tics but <em>formulae</em>:
    ready-made phrases metrically shaped to fill a line of dactylic hexameter (the
    epic's six-beat metre), letting a singer compose fluent, rapid verse in real time.
    Around the formulae hang <em>type-scenes</em> — the arming, the sacrifice, the
    arrival — assembled and varied like musical phrases.
  </p>
  <p>
    This observation was the twentieth century's great Homerological discovery. Milman
    Parry, in the 1920s and 1930s, showed that the formula system is too systematic to
    be literary habit and too necessary to be decoration — and tested the point by
    recording living oral poets in Yugoslavia, who composed epic in performance using
    exactly such traditional phraseology. His collaborator Albert Lord published the
    synthesis as <em>The Singer of Tales</em> (1960). The "Homeric question" — were the
    poems one genius's work or a composite? — was reframed: the Iliad and Odyssey are
    the crystallised distillation of a centuries-long singing tradition, unified by an
    extraordinary poet or poets, and fixed by writing at some point in the eighth to
    sixth centuries BCE (an ancient tradition credits Athens under the tyrant
    Pisistratus; scholars still argue). One performer within the Odyssey — the blind
    bard Demodocus, singing at a feast — is Homer's own picture of how these poems
    lived before they were books. Every statistical oddity of the text (line counts
    that run to 15,693 in the Iliad and 12,110 in the Odyssey, both in 24 books) is
    evidence about that oral life.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Oral composition changes how you read everything. When Achilles' shield is described
    for hundreds of lines, that is not padding but the tradition doing what it does
    brilliantly — ekphrasis, the virtuoso set-piece — and when a character's fate is
    foreshadowed repeatedly, that is how a tradition that expects its audience to know
    the story sustains tension anyway: you listen to hear <em>how</em>, not what. Ring
    composition (a passage returning to its starting point before moving on), extended
    similes that slide from battlefield to ordinary life, and scenes of ordinary people
    (the swineherd, the slave women, the old dog Argos who recognises his master and
    dies) are technique and moral vision at once. The poems also remember an older world
    imperfectly on purpose: Bronze Age palaces and boar's-tusk helmets surface in a poem
    where heroes are cremated (a later practice) and the Phoenicians trade abroad —
    archaeology confirms the mixture is real, a Dark Age poet's honest memory of a
    vanished world.
  </p>
  <p>
    The afterlife is the other half of the subject. Virgil built the Aeneid on the two
    poems' architecture (war epic and wandering epic); Dante made Virgil his guide;
    Joyce's Ulysses mapped a Dublin day onto the Odyssey; and the retellings continue —
    war poetry, novels, film — because the questions have not aged: what is worth dying
    for, what war does to those who fight it and those who wait, whether a home can
    survive its hero's return. Two millennia of translation debates (Lattimore's
    fidelity, Fagles' speed, and Wilson's flat-out claim to make the Odyssey new) are
    part of the tradition too: each generation re-sings the songs, which is, if Parry
    and Lord are right, exactly what the poems have always been for.
  </p>

